{% from 'networkpolicy.yml.j2' import networkpolicy with context %}
---
# Authelia's session store. authelia dials it at session.redis.host=authelia-redis
# (config-secret.yaml.j2), and nothing else does.
#
# THIS POLICY SHIPS WITH THE ROLE THAT NEEDS IT, and that is the whole point of the file's
# location. Kept in another role, `--tags authelia` would stage a portal that cannot reach
# its session store, and Authelia refuses to start without one: it exits on
# `error initializing session backend: redis connection error: ... i/o timeout` and
# CrashLoopBackOffs behind every forwardAuth route in the fleet. `build_k8s_dep_map` cannot
# see such a split — it derives a role's edges from templates inside that role's own
# directory, so a dependency expressed in a sibling role produces no edge and no ordering
# constraint to violate.
#
# 6379 is the containerPort AND the Service port, and
# tests/services/authelia/test_authelia_redis_sessions.py keeps them matching. That test also
# pins this file's co-location, so moving it fails the suite rather than the pod.
#
# The crowdsec agent sidecar shares the authelia pod, so it arrives here carrying `app:
# authelia` too. That is a property of pod-level identity, not a second rule to write — see
# "A sidecar carries its host pod's network identity" in project memory.
#
# NO `netpol_baseline_enforced` BRANCH, unlike the baseline's own policies. That lever is a
# role default of netpol-baseline, and Ansible role defaults are role-scoped: it does not
# resolve here, and `| default(true)` would read as honouring the lever while ignoring it.
# Dropping it is also the right behaviour rather than a concession. The lever exists to
# restore reachability in a rollback, and the only path that reaches redis at all is the one
# this file grants — so leaving it on cannot be what an operator is rolling back from.
#
# The port and caller list are the `netpol_fences` item for `authelia-redis` on this role's
# containers_list entry (cluster/services.yml). Its `role_owned: true` keeps
# netpol-baseline's callers loop from rendering a second copy. A missing item fails the render
# rather than shipping no policy.
{% set fence = container_item.netpol_fences | selectattr('app', 'equalto', 'authelia-redis') | first %}
{{ networkpolicy(fence.app, fence.port, fence['from']) }}
